Task 11 Pre-Implementation Report: Prototype Registration & Login

---
1. Current state

User schema (V2__core_schema.sql)
- users(id UUID PK, name VARCHAR NOT NULL, email VARCHAR NOT NULL UNIQUE, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ)
- Email uniqueness is already enforced at the database level (uq_users_email).
- There is no password_hash column, and none will be added.
- Email is stored without normalization in the current seeded data (dev@snapland.local, test@snapland.local).

WorkspaceMember schema
- workspace_member(id, workspace_id FK, user_id FK, role CHECK('OWNER','EDITOR','VIEWER'), UNIQUE(workspace_id, user_id))
- The relationship already exists and is the correct model to reuse.

DevProperties
- Binds app.dev.user-id and app.dev.workspace-id — a deterministic UUID pair that identifies the single seeded dev/test workspace. This is the existing configuration mechanism the prototype already uses to name the shared workspace.

DevDataInitializer / TestDataInitializer
- Both seed one user + one workspace + one OWNER membership using ON CONFLICT ... DO NOTHING. The workspace UUID comes from DevProperties.

Current dev token flow
Angular APP_INITIALIZER
    → POST /api/dev/token
    → JWT for fixed dev userId
    → stored in localStorage as "snapland_dev_token"
    → REST interceptor attaches Bearer header
    → RealtimeService reads token for STOMP connectHeaders
This is invisible to the user and supports only the one seeded identity.

DevTokenController
- @Profile("dev"), POST /dev/token, no credential check, issues JWT for devProperties.userId().
- Creates its own NimbusJwtEncoder inline using JwtProperties. Token issuance logic is duplicated relative to what registration/login will need.

Frontend auth state
- AuthService.bootstrap() automatically calls /dev/token when no token is in localStorage. This must stop being the normal browser flow.
- authInterceptor attaches Authorization







The Task 11 plan is approved with the following required corrections.

1. JwtIssuer must no longer use "snapland-dev" as the issuer.

Registration and login are now the normal prototype authentication path,
not development-only token issuance.

Move the issuer to authentication configuration, for example:

app.auth.jwt-issuer: snapland

JwtIssuer should use the configured issuer.

Keep:
    sub = User UUID

The same JwtIssuer may still be used by DevTokenController.

2. Do not make production-path AuthService depend on DevProperties.

The shared prototype workspace is now part of the prototype application
model, not development scaffolding.

Replace the misleading:

    app.dev.workspace-id

dependency in registration logic with an explicit prototype/application
configuration property, for example:

    app.prototype.workspace-id

Introduce an appropriately named configuration properties class such as
PrototypeProperties.

AuthService may depend on PrototypeProperties.

DevDataInitializer and TestDataInitializer may also use the same configured
prototype workspace ID where appropriate.

Do not leave production registration dependent on a @Profile("dev") bean.

3. The proposed frontend token guard behavior is acceptable for this MVP.

The guard may check only whether a token exists.

Document/confirm the resulting behavior:

    expired token exists
        -> /map may initially open
        -> first protected REST request returns 401
        -> interceptor clears token
        -> navigate to /auth

Do not add client-side JWT validation or refresh-token logic in this task.

